WEBVTT

00:00.320 --> 00:02.400
Alright, welcome to lecture 19.

00:03.320 --> 00:06.500
In this lecture, we'll derive the solution to the general cubic equation.

00:07.400 --> 00:13.680
So let's begin by reviewing the solution for the n equals 1 and n equals 2 polynomial equations.

00:13.900 --> 00:16.520
Alright, so n equals 1 we've been doing for a while.

00:16.720 --> 00:17.820
So that's just a linear equation.

00:17.940 --> 00:20.420
If you have ax plus b equals to 0, you want to find the solution.

00:21.280 --> 00:23.720
Well, solve for x, right? Just isolate x.

00:23.800 --> 00:25.120
And that worked pretty well, didn't it?

00:26.020 --> 00:26.960
Minus b over a.

00:26.960 --> 00:29.640
And you can do that as long as a is not equal to 0, right?

00:30.000 --> 00:31.900
Okay, n equals 2.

00:32.160 --> 00:34.500
Well, that's where we do completing the square, right?

00:34.620 --> 00:38.880
So we start with ax squared plus bx plus c for a non-zero.

00:39.640 --> 00:41.380
Factor out the a, right?

00:41.920 --> 00:44.140
And then complete the square.

00:44.500 --> 00:49.160
So we group all of the x terms together by putting them in a square.

00:49.820 --> 00:54.660
And that way we isolate x as a square equal to this quantity over here.

00:55.240 --> 00:58.600
And then we take the square root of both sides.

00:58.600 --> 01:03.940
And that gives us x plus b over 2a equals to plus or minus the square root of all this stuff, right?

01:04.500 --> 01:08.680
Now, as I discussed in our complex lecture,

01:09.560 --> 01:13.340
this still makes sense when a, b, and c are complex.

01:13.540 --> 01:17.800
It's just then you have to take the square root in the complex sense, right?

01:17.800 --> 01:23.180
Now, the technique of completing the square has sort of an analog for cubics.

01:23.340 --> 01:29.500
And I'll follow the presentation of Rotman and his text on Galois theory,

01:29.980 --> 01:32.480
second edition, pages 44, and so forth.

01:33.480 --> 01:37.900
So, Galois, excuse me, not Galois, Rotman is a prolific author.

01:38.020 --> 01:39.700
He wrote many books on mathematics.

01:39.700 --> 01:45.420
And, um, uh, the, um, you know, a lot of people write a lot of good math books.

01:45.540 --> 01:51.580
But you'll find, if you study Rotman's books, you'll notice that his presentation, it exceeds many others.

01:51.720 --> 01:56.220
It's, um, anyway, I have a lot of praise for Rotman.

01:56.300 --> 01:59.080
That's why you find me using Rotman here and there in different courses.

01:59.300 --> 02:00.600
But, anyway, moving on here.

02:01.060 --> 02:01.860
So how do we do the cubic?

02:01.860 --> 02:03.480
All right.

02:04.080 --> 02:09.320
Well, first of all, we need to take a step back here and get a general idea,

02:09.480 --> 02:12.340
which is to talk about a polynomial being depressed.

02:13.000 --> 02:13.560
All right.

02:15.140 --> 02:18.940
Although I think, um, I think Rotman uses the term reduced,

02:19.080 --> 02:21.140
but I've seen the word depressed used more generally.

02:21.840 --> 02:25.940
So a polynomial of degree n is depressed if it has no x to the n minus 1 term.

02:26.040 --> 02:28.620
So it skips over the next to leading order term.

02:28.620 --> 02:32.560
So it goes from n to n minus 2 when you look at the polynomial, all right?

02:33.360 --> 02:39.640
And it turns out that if we have a monic, that means leading coefficient is 1, polynomial, right?

02:40.200 --> 02:49.020
Then we can turn it into a depressed polynomial by making the substitution of big x equal to little x minus 1 over n, a sub n minus 1.

02:50.340 --> 02:55.200
So, like in the quadratic case, that's the completing the square trick, x minus 1 half b.

02:56.560 --> 02:58.220
That, make that substitution.

02:58.220 --> 03:02.060
Well, we'll see that down here at the bottom of the page, but, um, anyway, here's the lemma.

03:02.620 --> 03:08.780
If we replace big x with little x minus 1 over n times a to the n minus 1, that gets us a depressed polynomial.

03:09.100 --> 03:11.580
Let's call it f tilde of little x equal to that.

03:12.440 --> 03:16.980
Um, and moreover, if u is a root of f, u is a root of the depressed polynomial,

03:17.420 --> 03:23.160
then u minus 1 over, um, n times a to the n minus 1 is a root of the original polynomial.

03:23.160 --> 03:26.400
So, the proof is just a straightforward calculation.

03:26.720 --> 03:29.080
Let me scooch this up a bit here so you can see it.

03:29.720 --> 03:34.240
Um, proof is just a straight, straight calculation here.

03:34.580 --> 03:41.400
And, um, it's like, let's big x, big x equal to little x minus 1 over n minus 1.

03:42.140 --> 03:46.820
Plug it in here, plug it in here, plug it in there, expand those out, binomial theorem, right?

03:47.580 --> 03:56.260
Um, the next to leading term is n times x to the n minus 1 times, um, minus 1 over n, a sub n minus 1, right?

03:56.260 --> 04:01.160
And then the leading term in the second term is a to the n minus 1 times x to the n minus 1.

04:01.640 --> 04:10.440
So, this guy, right, this guy here, and this guy here, they cancel, right?

04:11.400 --> 04:12.320
These guys cancel.

04:13.120 --> 04:18.000
And that means that we have no next to leading order term in the substituted polynomial,

04:18.280 --> 04:20.260
which we define to be f tilde of x.

04:20.260 --> 04:26.580
Now, here, the constants, b sub n minus 2, da, da, da, da, da, b1 and b0,

04:26.660 --> 04:32.080
these are somewhat complicated and involve the original coefficients jumbled around a bit.

04:32.160 --> 04:38.860
And we'll see how these, these, these coefficients are, are, are, um, are given for specific case,

04:39.620 --> 04:45.260
just the specific case of n equals 2 and n equals 3, um, um, in this, in this, in this video.

04:45.260 --> 04:52.260
And then finally, if f tilde of u is equal to 0, then f tilde of x equals to that.

04:52.440 --> 05:03.560
So, um, that means that f of x is equal to replacing x with, um, f tilde with x plus 1 over n, a to the n minus 1.

05:04.800 --> 05:10.160
So anyway, you just sort through it, and you can see that f tilde of u is equal to 0 as, as desired.

05:10.160 --> 05:17.480
All right, now, um, well, not, this is given, what, what's, what's surprising, or not as well as surprising,

05:17.600 --> 05:22.680
but what's interesting is that the original polynomial at u minus 1 over n, a minus 1 is 0.

05:23.460 --> 05:29.620
So here's, here's the application of this general lemma to the n equals 2 case, all right,

05:29.700 --> 05:33.280
which, of course, we've already solved with completing the square, right?

05:33.280 --> 05:41.080
But this is a, is a slightly different, uh, kind of a tricky, um, uh, tricky technique that replaces,

05:41.180 --> 05:46.240
it's like completing the square except using a substitution is how I would, would describe it.

05:46.300 --> 05:52.040
But, um, so f big x, x squared plus bx plus c, this is the arbitrary monic quadratic.

05:52.700 --> 05:59.580
And then I substitute x minus 1 half b in for big x and square that thing.

05:59.580 --> 06:01.720
This defines f tilde, right?

06:02.460 --> 06:07.820
And when you square these things out, the bx terms cancel, and you're just left with x squared.

06:08.780 --> 06:15.400
Um, this c is that c, plus c, and then if you look at it, you get a, um, a plus 1 quarter b squared from this,

06:15.480 --> 06:17.680
and you get a minus 1 half b squared from that.

06:17.940 --> 06:21.160
So that gives you a net of minus 1 quarter b squared, all right?

06:21.860 --> 06:27.480
So, f tilde of x equal to 0, that's easy to solve, because that just gives you, u is,

06:27.480 --> 06:34.080
if u, if u is the solution, that gives you, u equal to plus or minus 1 half square root of b squared minus 4c.

06:34.900 --> 06:40.460
Um, I, I, oftentimes we wouldn't write u there, we'd just write little x, okay?

06:40.680 --> 06:45.900
But, um, anyway, um, those are the solutions to the depressed quadratic.

06:45.900 --> 06:53.040
And then, using the lemma, the solutions to the original quadratic are the depressed solutions minus, um,

06:54.180 --> 06:56.720
a sub n minus 1 over 2 in this case, which is minus b over 2.

06:56.800 --> 07:02.120
And, of course, we recognize that as the quadratic formula in the case a equals to 1.

